{
  "requirement": "When implementation shows that agreed requirement text must change, the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to stop work that depends on the change and take the changed requirements through review, agreement, and commit before continuing.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The tests adequately check `requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill.text_change`.\n\n**Obligations derived from the text.** When implementation shows that agreed text must change, the `habeas-requirements` skill must direct agents to do two things:\n1. Stop work that depends on the change.\n2. Take the changed requirements through review, agreement, and commit before continuing.\n\n**Test coverage.** `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_stops_dependent_work_when_agreed_text_must_change]` is marked `{test}` for this requirement. It makes two checks.\n\n- **Directive phrase.** It calls `assert_phrases(\"habeas-requirements\", ...)` with the sentence \"If implementation shows that agreed text must change, stop work that depends on it and take the changed requirements through steps 3 to 5 first\".\n  - `assert_phrases` runs `assert_text` over `bundled_skill`, which collapses whitespace in the shipped `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md` and its reference files. The phrase therefore matches even though it is wrapped across lines in the Workflow section.\n  - The sentence is present in that section.\n  - It covers both obligations: stopping dependent work, and taking the changes through the later steps \"first\", meaning before continuing.\n- **Step meanings.** It asserts through `step(...)` that step 3 starts with \"Review.\", step 4 with \"Present\", and step 5 with \"Commit.\". In the Workflow section, step 3 is the subagent review, step 4 presents the text to the user and gets their agreement, and step 5 commits. \"Steps 3 to 5\" therefore means review, agreement, and commit, as the requirement requires. If steps were renumbered or reordered, the test would fail.\n\n**Gaps.** I found none. The requirement constrains the skill's text, so checking that text and the step mapping is the right kind of test. I saw no other scenario or boundary the requirement names that is left untested. No `{fixme}` acknowledgments apply.",
